WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.340
Dependencies extend the trust boundary of an application.

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:15.640
A package may be well maintained, widely used, and still introduce breaking changes, vulnerabilities, unexpected behavior, or transitive dependencies the team did not directly choose.

3
00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:21.580
Defensive TypeScript teams treat dependency decisions as engineering decisions, not just installation steps.

4
00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:28.820
Useful defenses include lockfiles, minimal packages, reviewed updates, and dependency scanning where appropriate.

5
00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.200
Minimal packages do not mean never using libraries.

6
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:40.460
They mean avoiding unnecessary dependencies for trivial work and understanding the operational cost of each package that becomes part of the application.

7
00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.540
Configuration should be validated at startup.

8
00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:50.440
Environment variables, feature flags, service endpoints, timeouts, and mode settings are runtime inputs.

9
00:00:50.540 --> 00:01:02.220
If a required value is missing or malformed, it is usually better to fail clearly during startup than to fail unpredictably after the system has accepted traffic or processed partial work.

10
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:05.060
Secrets require special care.

11
00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:10.440
They should not be hardcoded, logged, committed, or exposed to client-side bundles.

12
00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:20.300
A defensive build process makes it clear which values are server-only, which values may be public, and how secret rotation or missing secret behavior is handled.

13
00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.460
Build settings should support production-safe behavior.

14
00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.120
Type checking should run before deployment.

15
00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.980
Linting, when available, can catch risky patterns.

16
00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:41.200
Production builds should not depend on development-only assumptions, debug flags, permissive mock behavior, or local environment details that are absent in deployed systems.

17
00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:46.920
Defensive teams make risky assumptions visible in configuration and deployment processes.

18
00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:54.140
They document required variables, validate them early, review dependency changes, and keep build behavior consistent.

19
00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:00.320
This reduces incidents caused by hidden operational assumptions rather than application logic alone.
